WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.960
So we've talked about CDNs,

00:01.960 --> 00:03.800
we've talked about working with just the index.html,

00:03.800 --> 00:06.980
but most projects are not going to be run that way.

00:06.980 --> 00:07.940
That's just the reality.

00:07.940 --> 00:11.320
Most people have bought in fully to the tool chain system,

00:11.320 --> 00:13.840
and so it's time that we did so too.

00:13.840 --> 00:15.600
For those who come from the Vue2 ecosystem,

00:15.600 --> 00:18.160
you're probably used to scaffolding your app with Vue CLI.

00:18.160 --> 00:20.480
But there's been a new kid on the block in terms of

00:20.480 --> 00:22.180
the whole webpack build tool chain,

00:22.180 --> 00:25.520
and if you haven't heard about it, it's called Vue.

00:26.000 --> 00:28.120
One of the great things about Vue,

00:28.120 --> 00:30.680
basically what you need to know is that it's a bundler underneath

00:30.680 --> 00:34.720
that's helping to push front-end tooling to use more ES modules.

00:34.720 --> 00:37.920
Basically, it helps to make apps more performant.

00:37.920 --> 00:40.440
What's really nice about Vue though, as well,

00:40.440 --> 00:43.080
is that even though EvanU, the creator of Vue,

00:43.080 --> 00:46.040
is one of the founders of the Vue technology,

00:46.040 --> 00:48.160
it's a multi-framework tooling.

00:48.160 --> 00:51.360
So it's really nice to see a lot of frameworks starting to gather around it.

00:51.360 --> 00:54.080
So for anyone who's interested in this kind of stuff and want to see

00:54.080 --> 00:57.160
like helping to create plugins so that other frameworks can leverage that,

00:57.160 --> 01:00.000
Vue is doing a very good job as far as bringing the community together.

01:00.000 --> 01:02.600
But because of all these performance optimizations though,

01:02.600 --> 01:06.360
Vue CLI currently still uses a webpack and so it basically is being deprecated in

01:06.360 --> 01:13.440
favor of the createVue which is the new basically equivalent of Vue CLI.

01:13.440 --> 01:17.760
For this one, this has Vue powering it underneath.

01:17.760 --> 01:19.600
So this is where the shift is.

01:19.600 --> 01:23.440
So going forward, if you're creating a new Vue 3 app and you have

01:23.440 --> 01:25.600
the luxury of using Vue underneath,

01:25.600 --> 01:29.040
createVue is the basically recommended way going forward.

01:29.040 --> 01:33.840
So let's go ahead and create a project and see what that looks like, shall we?

01:33.840 --> 01:39.200
To do that, we're going to run the command NPM createVue3 inside of our project.

01:39.200 --> 01:42.880
So I'm going to go ahead and just close some of this off now,

01:42.880 --> 01:45.480
and let's go ahead and open this up.

01:45.480 --> 01:49.520
NPM createVue at 3.

01:49.520 --> 01:51.840
So one of the things that's really nice about

01:51.840 --> 01:55.240
the modern built tool chains these days is that you don't have to

01:55.240 --> 01:58.960
install a massive CLI on your computer only to use it a couple of times,

01:58.960 --> 02:00.640
maybe like a year or something.

02:00.640 --> 02:03.680
It's being called on the fly because NPM knows,

02:03.680 --> 02:05.480
you want to temporarily install something,

02:05.480 --> 02:06.920
I'll basically bring down the package,

02:06.920 --> 02:10.960
I'll use it and after that it won't be on your machine anymore.

