02:17.560 --> 02:20.920
an opportunity to create your own building on what we've been doing today.

02:20.920 --> 02:25.400
All right, so I've named it Sandbox,

02:25.400 --> 02:27.880
and so at first glance, you see at TypeScript,

02:27.880 --> 02:30.600
your first impression might be,

02:30.600 --> 02:32.560
I'm definitely not using TypeScript, hit no.

02:32.560 --> 02:34.560
I'm going to say hit yes,

02:34.560 --> 02:36.520
and the short reason for that is

02:36.520 --> 02:39.720
because when Vue gives you the option to add the TypeScript,

02:39.720 --> 02:42.240
what it's saying is it's not saying you have to use TypeScript,

02:42.240 --> 02:45.600
it's saying we're going to configure everything so that if you ever choose to use TypeScript,

02:45.720 --> 02:48.520
it's really easy, no configuration needed kind of it.

02:48.520 --> 02:51.320
There'll be one or two lines of code we'll add to make it basically

02:51.320 --> 02:53.400
like ignore any TypeScript errors,

02:53.400 --> 02:55.280
but one of the things that I found that's really

02:55.280 --> 02:57.360
painful when it comes to code migrations is that

02:57.360 --> 02:59.880
people who want to start sprinkling in TypeScript,

02:59.880 --> 03:02.800
usually the build tool chain is what blocks them from doing that because they're like,

03:02.800 --> 03:04.840
oh, I just want to try types and then it's like,

03:04.840 --> 03:06.400
oh, you have to look at this config file,

03:06.400 --> 03:09.080
then you have to do a TS config and then by the time they've looked like

03:09.080 --> 03:13.120
that obstacle it's getting started can be super expensive.

