13:16.400 --> 13:19.120
allow us to basically identify common problems

13:19.120 --> 13:20.960
that people sort of, or mistakes that they make

13:20.960 --> 13:22.440
when it comes to this stuff.

13:22.440 --> 13:24.920
And so you'll notice here, we got these error messages here

13:24.920 --> 13:27.080
because it's saying that whenever we iterate

13:27.080 --> 13:28.640
through an element, it's expected to have

13:28.640 --> 13:30.440
a vbind key directive.

13:30.440 --> 13:32.040
And so what does that mean exactly?

13:32.040 --> 13:35.120
Well, basically when you're having the frameworks

13:35.120 --> 13:37.200
track the elements that they're looping through,

13:37.200 --> 13:39.000
it's good to give them some sort of identifier

13:39.000 --> 13:41.360
so that they know whether or not something's changed or not.

13:41.360 --> 13:43.760
Without that identifier, you basically run the risk

13:43.800 --> 13:47.120
of relying solely on the data to figure out what's working.

13:47.120 --> 13:49.240
And especially when it comes to animation,

13:49.240 --> 13:51.920
it'll bite you in the butt constantly.

13:51.920 --> 13:53.540
And so typically what people do then

13:53.540 --> 13:55.080
is they will do the vbind directive

13:55.080 --> 13:58.120
because the key needs to be a unique key for everything.

13:58.120 --> 13:59.800
And so we can also shortcut this as well

13:59.800 --> 14:01.960
to just like this, vkey.

14:01.960 --> 14:04.960
And then we can do something as simple as, for example,

14:04.960 --> 14:07.680
I sometimes would just take out the index

14:07.680 --> 14:13.160
and I'll say I will do a ES6 string,

14:13.160 --> 14:16.320
and I'll go item-index, for example.

14:18.160 --> 14:20.920
And so this here then basically provides a custom key.

14:20.920 --> 14:22.360
Now most of the time when you're working with databases,

14:22.360 --> 14:24.040
hopefully you have UUIDs and that kind of thing

14:24.040 --> 14:26.760
to populate it, but for the sake of like

14:26.760 --> 14:28.520
sort of demoing this at the moment,

14:28.520 --> 14:30.400
this is how we could fulfill this requirement.

14:30.400 --> 14:31.880
They're basically creating our own key.

14:31.880 --> 14:35.840
So you'll see here, it notices that this is,

14:35.840 --> 14:40.840
I think identical to the other one, but index, it doesn't.

14:40.880 --> 14:45.200
So there you go, number, index, great.

14:45.200 --> 14:46.280
What does it not like about it?

14:46.280 --> 14:47.520
Oh, it'll format it.

14:47.520 --> 14:49.280
Okay, there we go.

14:51.000 --> 14:53.360
And just like that, we have our app now working

14:53.360 --> 14:55.920
inside of the build tool chain.

14:55.920 --> 14:57.560
This is the final bit that I was talking about.

14:57.560 --> 14:58.840
Remember how I was talking about

14:58.840 --> 15:02.000
where we've enabled TypeScript, but you don't have to use it?

15:02.000 --> 15:03.160
The main thing here is that you'll notice

15:03.160 --> 15:04.760
Volar is basically saying, hey,

15:04.760 --> 15:07.160
I thought we were in TypeScript, what's going on?

15:07.160 --> 15:11.160
So what you gotta do here is go into your tsconfig.json

15:11.160 --> 15:14.840
and under the compiler options, we can allow js to true.

15:15.780 --> 15:17.960
And that'll basically prevent it from yelling at us anymore

15:17.960 --> 15:20.480
if we don't use TypeScript.

15:20.480 --> 15:22.560
But on the other hand, if anyone ever does use TypeScript,

15:22.560 --> 15:24.360
it's just gonna work magically.

15:24.360 --> 15:26.320
And all the configurations already work.

15:26.320 --> 15:27.160
Oh yes, go.

15:27.160 --> 15:28.520
If you have an existing project,

15:28.520 --> 15:32.640
would you probably not recommend trying to vitifize it?

15:34.240 --> 15:35.160
So the question here is around,

15:35.160 --> 15:36.800
if you have an existing project that already works,

15:36.840 --> 15:38.960
whether or not to switch it over to vit.

15:38.960 --> 15:42.240
Honestly, most of the people who've switched to tool chain

15:42.240 --> 15:43.840
have found very little difference.

15:43.840 --> 15:46.960
In terms of like, it's not like other build tools

15:46.960 --> 15:49.220
where like something usually major breaks.

15:49.220 --> 15:50.480
So I guess a lot of it boils down

15:50.480 --> 15:52.040
to actually your dependencies.

15:52.040 --> 15:52.960
I would take a look at that.

15:52.960 --> 15:55.100
If you have a lot of dependencies that rely

15:55.100 --> 15:57.120
on some sort of webpack build,

15:57.120 --> 16:00.000
then in that case, vit might be a little premature.

16:00.000 --> 16:01.540
But if it's a more evergreen project

16:01.540 --> 16:03.320
where it has like standard JavaScript stuff

16:03.320 --> 16:06.240
that it's JavaScript libraries installing

16:06.240 --> 16:09.240
that might already have ESM support for ES modules,

16:09.240 --> 16:11.000
then that would be a great case for like,

16:11.000 --> 16:12.200
let's just try and see what happens

16:12.200 --> 16:13.840
because the performance improvements

16:13.840 --> 16:16.280
that people are experiencing on vit are pretty significant.

16:16.280 --> 16:19.160
And so it's honestly, it's worth,

16:19.160 --> 16:21.040
it's worth like an hour time box effort

16:21.040 --> 16:23.640
just to see if you can improve the DX on that regard.

